  "narrative": "All estimates are interpreted exactly as signed to the claim direction; for these direction -1 dependency claims, a positive estimate means the exposed group has a more negative dependency score, i.e. stronger dependency, while a negative estimate means the opposite. H15 is reconfirmed at its response deadline. The claim is that TP53-wild-type cells have stronger USP7 dependency than TP53-loss cells. Discovery gave +0.802 with a 95% discovery interval of +0.782 to +0.822, and independent validation gave +0.799 with independent interval +0.769 to +0.828. The two estimates are nearly identical, and the validation interval remains far above zero. This is a very large, reproducible dataset-level association, so I accept H15 and keep it closed. H6 is now fully negative. KRAS mutation versus KRAS-intact status for dependency_KRAS was +0.014 in discovery (-0.004 to +0.031) and, with independent validation, -0.001 (-0.026 to +0.025). The validation interval includes zero and the point estimate is essentially null, so I reject H6 and close it. The key new validation is H92, the IFN-gamma-high context interaction for PTEN loss and STAT3 dependency. Discovery supported stronger PTEN-loss STAT3 dependency in the high-IFN context relative to its complement: +0.212 with a 95% discovery interval of +0.094 to +0.331. Independent validation, however, was much smaller and imprecise: +0.041 with independent interval -0.140 to +0.222. The validation interval includes zero and even includes a modest reverse effect, although it also remains compatible with a positive interaction through its upper bound. Thus the discovery is not replicated well enough to remain accepted, but the validation is not decisive enough to declare a strong rejection. I therefore downgrade H92 to unresolved and keep it active. This changes the interpretation of H91: the high-IFN main effect of PTEN loss on STAT3 dependency (+0.423, interval +0.319 to +0.526) remains an accepted dataset-level subgroup association, but we should not present the high-IFN interaction as validated context modification. The supported validated core associations remain SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency (H12: discovery +0.784, validation +0.800), MSI-high status with stronger WRN dependency overall (H16: discovery +0.790, validation +0.807) and in adenocarcinoma (H21: discovery +0.784, validation +0.813), TP53-wild-type status with stronger USP7 dependency (H15 as above), KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency (H17: discovery +0.796, validation +0.801), and PTEN loss with stronger STAT3 dependency overall (H81: discovery +0.267, validation +0.310), reinforced by context checks H84-H87 and H90. The most useful negative and specificity results are that PTEN loss is not meaningfully associated with dependency_KRAS (H89), dependency_MDM2 (H93), or dependency_USP7 (H94); PIK3CA mutation does not support a clear interaction (H88); KRAS mutation is not associated with STAT3 dependency (H95); and KRAS mutation shows no validated KRAS-dependency association (H6). These findings reduce the concern that PTEN loss is a broad dependency-altering state and strengthen the specificity of the PTEN/STAT3 main finding. Remaining unresolved uncertainty centers on whether the PTEN/STAT3 effect is truly enhanced by inflammatory IFN-gamma-high context or whether the discovery interaction was fragile due to the relatively small high-IFN subgroup. The most informative next analysis is to characterize the PTEN/STAT3 effect in lower-IFN contexts and with a broader IFN split. I therefore registered two follow-up claims: a PTEN-loss versus PTEN-intact main contrast in the lower top-quartile IFN complement, and a new IFN interaction using a median-like split above zero. If the low-IFN effect remains similar to the overall effect, the failed top-quartile interaction would be more plausibly due to instability or power limitations rather than true absence of context modification; if it is near zero, H91 would be better interpreted as a subgroup-specific discovery without validated interaction.",
  "research_notes": "Updated accepted/validated core associations: H12 SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency; H16/H21 MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency overall and in adenocarcinoma; H15 TP53 wild-type -> stronger USP7 dependency; H17 KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency; H81 PTEN loss -> stronger STAT3 dependency with robustness checks H84-H87 and H90. H92 high-IFN PTEN/STAT3 interaction is now unresolved after failed independent validation: discovery +0.212 but validation +0.041 with interval -0.140 to +0.222. H91 remains accepted as a high-IFN subgroup main-effect discovery, but context specificity is not validated. H6 KRAS mutation -> KRAS dependency is reject/closed after validation null. Next priority: test PTEN/STAT3 in lower IFN context and with a broader IFN interaction split to resolve whether the high-IFN effect modification is real but fragile or overfit/underpowered. All conclusions remain internal dataset associations, not causal or externally generalizable claims."
