  "narrative": "All estimates are interpreted exactly as signed to the registered hypothesis direction. For direction -1 main contrasts, a positive estimate means the exposed group has a stronger, more negative dependency score than the comparator; for direction +1 main contrasts, a positive estimate means weaker dependency. For direction -1 interactions, a positive estimate would mean the exposed-group contrast is stronger in the named subgroup than in its complement.   H2 (R108), H5 (R109), and H11 (R110) tested MYC amplification on DHX9, KIF18A, and RPL11 dependency. The signed estimates were -0.003, +0.006, and -0.012 respectively, all close to zero and all interval-compatible with no effect. These are far below the practical null scale I am using for dependency scores and do not support MYC amplification as a main predictor of these three outcomes in this dataset. I reject and close all three.   H121 (R111), H122 (R112), H123 (R113), and H124 (R114) tested specificity or pleiotropy of accepted axes. PTEN loss had essentially no association with ERBB2 dependency (-0.002; interval -0.026 to +0.022) or EGFR dependency (+0.006; interval -0.017 to +0.029). KEAP1 loss had only a small positive association with STAT3 dependency (+0.016; interval -0.012 to +0.044), and TP53 wild-type versus loss had only a small positive association with STAT3 dependency (+0.012; interval -0.012 to +0.037). None are statistically significant or practically meaningful, and none undermine the accepted PTEN-loss/STAT3, KEAP1-loss/NFE2L2, or TP53-status axes. I reject and close these specificity checks.   H125 (R115) tested whether KRAS mutation is associated with stronger BRAF dependency among BRAF wild-type cells. The estimate was +0.015 with interval -0.003 to +0.034. It is small and not statistically supported. H126 (R116) tested whether BRAF V600E is associated with weaker RIT1 dependency among KRAS wild-type cells; the signed estimate was +0.013 with interval -0.021 to +0.047, again null. I reject and close both MAPK-bypass relationships.   H127 (R117) and H128 (R118) tested cognate receptor dependencies among adenocarcinoma cells that are KRAS wild-type. ERBB2 amplification gave a small negative signed estimate -0.020 with interval -0.058 to +0.018; EGFR amplification gave +0.019 with interval -0.018 to +0.055. Neither reaches a meaningful magnitude or excludes zero. I reject and close both. These negative results remain dataset-level specificity checks, not proof that receptor amplification cannot matter in other settings.   The key new result is H132 (R119): CDKN2A loss versus no CDKN2A loss on MDM2 dependency. Under the registered direction -1 hypothesis of stronger MDM2 dependency in CDKN2A-loss cells, the signed estimate is +0.117 with 95% interval +0.082 to +0.152. This corresponds to an approximately 0.117 score-unit shift toward more negative MDM2 dependency in CDKN2A-loss cells. On the MDM2 outcome scale, this is roughly a 40-50% difference relative to the overall mean absolute dependency magnitude and about half a standard deviation, so it is much larger than the near-null effects seen elsewhere in this iteration and crosses the 10% practical-significance threshold by the stated convention.   However, this is still a new discovery after many comparisons and has clear potential confounders. CDKN2A deletion lies in a 9p21 locus that often co-occurs with MTAP deletion, and CDKN2A status is correlated with TP53 pathway alterations; MDM2 biology is strongly p53-pathway-related. Therefore I do not accept H132 immediately. I request independent validation of H132 and register follow-up tests stratified by TP53 status, including the effect of TP53 wild-type versus loss on MDM2 within CDKN2A contexts; MTAP-loss main effect and CDKN2A effect stratified by MTAP status; CDKN2A effect among higher-Cas9 screens to reduce screen-quality confounding; and a specificity test of CDKN2A loss on USP7 dependency, expected potentially opposite if the axis is tied to p53 pathway status. H132 is kept unresolved and active pending validation and follow-up.   The lower-priority untested claims H129, H130, H131, and H133 are deferred because the CDKN2A-MDM2 signal, if validated, is more informative for allocation of remaining work. All conclusions remain dataset-level associations, not causal or externally generalizable claims.",
  "research_notes": "Core accepted dataset associations: SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency; MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency across overall and several contexts; TP53 wild-type -> stronger USP7 dependency; KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency; PTEN loss -> stronger STAT3 dependency broadly and in many contexts including PIK3CA-mutant cells. Interaction evidence remains weak overall.   Iteration 9 new negative/specificty results closed: MYC amplification is not associated with DHX9, KIF18A, or RPL11 dependency overall; PTEN loss has no meaningful effect on ERBB2 or EGFR dependency; KEAP1 loss and TP53 wild-type have no meaningful main effect on STAT3 dependency; KRAS mutation does not show BRAF dependency in BRAF wild-type cells; BRAF V600E does not alter RIT1 dependency in KRAS wild-type cells; ERBB2 and EGFR amplification do not show cognate dependencies in adenocarcinoma KRAS wild-type cells.   H132 is the key new candidate: CDKN2A loss -> stronger MDM2 dependency, signed +0.117 (95% CI +0.082 to +0.152), cell counts 401 vs 1599. Status unresolved, investigation active; independent validation requested. Follow-up registered: CDKN2A effect by TP53 WT/loss and TP53 interaction; TP53 effect on MDM2 within CDKN2A strata; MTAP loss main effect and CDKN2A effect by MTAP status/interaction; CDKN2A effect among cas9_activity_pct >= 67.7; CDKN2A specificity on USP7 with direction +1 expecting possible weaker USP7 dependency. Lower-priority untested H129-H133 deferred. All conclusions remain dataset-level associations, not causal or externally generalizable.",
