gical hypothesis but an analysis-coding mismatch. These claims used continuous thresholds as exposed/comparator values, but the Welch cell-count diagnostic appears to evaluate exact-value exposure/comparator cells, giving exposed or comparator groups with zero or fewer than 20 observations. Therefore no scientific estimate exists. I keep them unresolved because they are not evidence against the biological hypotheses, but close them as not testable in the current exact-cell mean_difference framework. The same coding issue applies to H244-H246, direct continuous-threshold main effects for growth rate, so I close those as well. H247-H250 remain useful because they use thresholds as eligibility/subgroup conditions and binary exposures, not as exact-value main-effect contrasts.\n\nI also close H222-H225, arbitrary research-signature modifier interactions, because these constructed assays have no assigned gene/pathway and the prior probability of a scientifically meaningful effect modifier is low relative to the multiplicity already incurred. This is not a rejection based on effect direction; it is a resource allocation and inference-control decision. The accepted evidence base is already broad, and continuing arbitrary-signature interaction tests would primarily add multiplicity risk.\n\nOverall, the newly delivered valid results strengthen the accepted context robustness panel: the accepted axes persist in KRAS-mutant context and in a lower screen-quality stratum. The invalid continuous direct-predictor tests should not be interpreted as null evidence. No validation is requested because voluntary validation capacity is exhausted. Next, I register lower-ploidy context refinements for USP7, STAT3, MDM2, and WRN to complement the active lower-ploidy SMARCA2 and NFE2L2 tests. These are more informative than additional continuous-threshold direct effects that cannot be tested under the current cell-count convention.",
