  "narrative": "All newly delivered estimates are interpreted exactly as signed to the registered hypothesis direction; no additional sign multiplication is applied. For direction -1 claims, a positive signed estimate means the raw exposed-minus-comparator dependency score is more negative, i.e. stronger dependency in the exposed group. For the KEAP1-loss NFE2L2 direction +1 claim, a positive signed estimate means the exposed group has a less negative dependency score, i.e. weaker dependency.  \n\nThe accepted-axis robustness panel is strengthened. H247/R218 tests CDKN2A loss versus MDM2 dependency among faster-growing lines and gives +0.090 (95% CI +0.038 to +0.143). Because direction is -1, this means CDKN2A-loss lines have raw stronger MDM2 dependency than CDKN2A-intact lines in the fast-growth eligibility. The interval excludes the null, the point estimate is comparable to the accepted main effect H132/R119 (+0.117) and lower-quality screen-quality strata, and the lower bound is above the approximate 10% relative-difference relevance benchmark on the dependency-score scale. I accept H247 as a dataset-specific fast-growth context result and close it. Residual concerns remain that fast-growth eligibility is selected on a measured proliferation proxy and may be correlated with culture, screen, or genomic context, but the effect is sufficiently large and consistent to support persistence of the CDKN2A-MDM2 association in this subset.  \n\nH248/R219 tests PTEN loss versus STAT3 dependency within high-AXL expression eligibility and gives +0.286 (CI +0.177 to +0.395). This is direction -1, so it indicates raw stronger STAT3 dependency in PTEN-loss lines even in the AXL-high context. The magnitude is similar to or larger than the main PTEN-STAT3 effect and validation, the interval excludes the null comfortably, and it exceeds the relative-size relevance threshold. I accept H248 and close it. This supports, rather than undermines, the accepted PTEN-STAT3 axis in a resistance-like expression context.  \n\nH249-H254/R220-R225 complete the lower-ploidy robustness panel. All signed estimates are large and in the expected direction: SMARCA4-loss SMARCA2 +0.792 (CI +0.752 to +0.833); KEAP1-loss weaker NFE2L2 +0.776 (CI +0.736 to +0.817); TP53-wild-type stronger USP7 +0.801 (CI +0.762 to +0.841); PTEN-loss stronger STAT3 +0.193 (CI +0.094 to +0.292); CDKN2A-loss stronger MDM2 +0.117 (CI +0.042 to +0.192); MSI-high stronger WRN +0.788 (CI +0.737 to +0.838). Every interval excludes the null in the registered direction, and the magnitudes are well above the 10% relevance benchmark. I accept all six lower-ploidy context claims and close them. These are not independent external validations; they are in-dataset context tests. However, given the effect sizes for SMARCA2, USP7, NFE2L2, and WRN, the dominant scientific interpretation is persistence of the axes under lower-ploidy eligibility. For PTEN-STAT3 and CDKN2A-MDM2, the lower-ploidy estimates are somewhat smaller than some high-ploidy or global estimates but remain positive, statistically distinct from null, and above the relevance threshold.  \n\nThe direct PI3K-pathway hypotheses are not supported. H255/R226, H256/R227, and H257/R228 test whether PIK3CA mutation in KRAS-wildtype lines is associated with weaker EGFR, ERBB2, or KRAS dependency under direction +1. Signed estimates are +0.002 (CI -0.023 to +0.027), +0.011 (CI -0.014 to +0.036), and -0.001 (CI -0.026 to +0.025), respectively. All intervals include the null and point estimates are negligible on the dependency-score scale; they do not provide evidence for a clinically meaningful relative difference. H258/R229 tests whether PIK3CA mutation among TP53-wildtype lines is associated with stronger MDM2 dependency under direction -1 and gives +0.001 (CI -0.029 to +0.031), also null. I reject H255-H258 and close them as not supported. Competing explanations include imperfect binary coding of PI3K-pathway activation, heterogeneity of KRAS-wildtype or TP53-wildtype subsets, dependence of RTK/RAS dependency on upstream pathway features not captured by PIK3CA mutation, and the possibility that PIK3CA mutation is too sparse or biologically weak to shift these dependency scores in this dataset. These rejections are dataset-specific negative associations, not claims about lack of biological bypass mechanisms in general.  \n\nOverall, this batch strengthens the accepted robustness evidence for the six main dataset-specific axes and fails to support new PIK3CA-based direct-predictor hypotheses. No validation request is made because voluntary validation capacity is exhausted.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 18 appraise: All due discovery evidence was interpreted as already signed to the registered direction. Accepted axes are now robust in several additional contexts: CDKN2A-loss stronger MDM2 in fast-growth lines H247; PTEN-loss stronger STAT3 in high-AXL lines H248; and the lower-ploidy panel H249-H254 for SMARCA4-loss stronger SMARCA2, KEAP1-loss weaker NFE2L2, TP53-wild-type stronger USP7, PTEN-loss stronger STAT3, CDKN2A-loss stronger MDM2, and MSI-high stronger WRN. All accepted effects exclude null and exceed the practical 10% relative-difference benchmark, especially the large four axes; PTEN-STAT3 and CDKN2A-MDM2 are intermediate but consistent with main replicated effects. Direct PIK3CA tests H255-H258 are rejected: PIK3CA mutation does not support weaker EGFR/ERBB2/KRAS dependency in KRAS-wildtype lines or stronger MDM2 dependency in TP53-wildtype lines. These negative results use valid binary-exposure designs and should be read as dataset-specific non-significant associations, not biological refutations of PI3K pathway logic. Voluntary validation capacity remains exhausted. Future analysis should avoid repeating arbitrary-signature interactions or invalid exact-threshold continuous main effects; if used, focus on remaining untested high-value context questions with valid designs or accept that the core evidence base is sufficiently established."
