{
  "narrative": "No assessments are due, and no new validation response deadline or newly released validation result is present in this synthesis step. Voluntary validation capacity is exhausted, so I make no new validation request and no claim-status changes. The controller-maintained accepted set is unchanged. All estimates quoted below are already signed to the registered hypothesis direction. For direction -1 dependency claims, positive signed estimate means raw more-negative dependency, i.e. stronger dependency, in the exposed or TP53-intact comparator coding. For direction +1 NFE2L2 claims, positive signed estimate means raw less-negative dependency, i.e. weaker dependency_NFE2L2, in KEAP1-loss lines.\\n\\nSupported findings. In this supplied 2000-line lung-adenocarcinoma-predominant CRISPR dataset, six associations are scientifically meaningful, large relative to the 10% practical benchmark, internally robust across measured contexts, and supported by independent in-dataset validation: (1) SMARCA4 loss is associated with stronger SMARCA2 dependency: H12/R9 signed +0.784, 95% +0.765 to +0.803; validation R13 +0.800, +0.768 to +0.832; replicated across adenocarcinoma, ARID1A-intact, KRAS-mutant, MTAP-loss, ploidy, growth-rate, screen-quality and serum contexts. (2) MSI-high is associated with stronger WRN dependency: H16/R12 +0.790, +0.760 to +0.819; validation R39 +0.807, +0.759 to +0.855; replicated in adenocarcinoma, mutation-burden halves, ploidy, growth-rate, screen-quality and serum contexts. (3) TP53-intact/wild-type status, coded as tp53_loss=0 versus tp53_loss=1, is associated with stronger USP7 dependency: H15/R11 +0.802, +0.782 to +0.822; validation R53 +0.799, +0.769 to +0.828; replicated in adenocarcinoma, KRAS-mutant, KEAP1-loss, MTAP-loss, ploidy, growth-rate, Cas9, screen-quality and serum contexts. (4) KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency: H17/R6 +0.796, +0.776 to +0.816; validation R26 +0.800, +0.772 to +0.829; replicated in TP53 strata, adenocarcinoma, KRAS-mutant, ploidy, MTAP-loss, growth-rate, screen-quality, hypoxia and serum contexts. (5) PTEN loss is associated with stronger STAT3 dependency: H81/R63 +0.267, +0.215 to +0.319; validation R66 +0.310, +0.231 to +0.388; robust across batch, Cas9, culture, lineage, PIK3CA, IFN-gamma, TP53, KRAS, growth-rate, EMT/AXL, ploidy and serum contexts. (6) CDKN2A loss is associated with stronger MDM2 dependency: H132/R119 +0.117, +0.082 to +0.152; validation R120 +0.100, +0.049 to +0.151; robust across TP53, MTAP, KRAS, ploidy/aneuploidy, growth-rate, screen-quality and serum contexts. The serum-context claims accepted in the preceding appraise, including H279-H287, confirm that these six axes are not limited to one culture-medium state; low-serum SMARCA4-loss SMARCA2 interactions H288/R263 and MSI-high WRN interactions H289/R264 are rejected, but these interaction rejections do not refute the large accepted main effects.\\n\\nNegative findings. The dataset rejects many direct oncogene/tumor-suppressor dependency hypotheses and several secondary predictors. BRAF V600E does not support stronger BRAF dependency, H1/R1 and H179/R154. KRAS mutation does not support stronger KRAS dependency, H6/R4, with near-null validation R80. EGFR amplification does not support stronger EGFR dependency, H3/R2, H18/R14, H33/R24, and H128/R118. ERBB2 amplification does not support stronger ERBB2 or TMED10 dependency, H4/R3, H14/R25, and H127/R117. MYC amplification does not support stronger DHX9, KIF18A, POLR2A, RPL11, or MDM2 dependency, H2/R108, H5/R109, H9/R7, H11/R110, and H214/R188. TP53 loss alone does not support stronger MDM2 dependency, H7/R5, H143/R23, and H144/R248. Cross-receptor and co-oncogene hypotheses are largely negative: ERBB2 amplification does not predict EGFR dependency in EGFR-amplification-negative lines, H264/R235; EGFR amplification does not predict ERBB2 dependency in ERBB2-amplification-negative lines, H265/R236; PIK3CA mutation does not support EGFR, ERBB2, KRAS, or MDM2 dependency hypotheses, H255/R226, H256/R227, H257/R228, and H258/R229. PTEN loss does not support WRN, RPL11, EGFR, ERBB2, KRAS, or MDM2 dependency, H219/R191, H190/R171, H122/R112, H121/R111, H89/R72, and H93/R76. KEAP1 loss does not support WRN or USP7 dependency, H218/R190 and H74/R56. ARID1A loss does not support SMARCA2 or WRN dependency, H19/R15 and H235/R205. RB1 loss does not support MDM2 or KIF18A dependency, H191/R172 and H116/R102. Constructed research markers D, E, and F show no meaningful association, H45/R35 through H48/R38.\\n\\nUnresolved uncertainty. Several secondary or modifier signals are not accepted. Interaction or subgroup claims that were discovery-positive but not validated or were validated with null intervals remain unresolved: H92/R75 with validation R79; H103/R82 with validation R93; H114/R100 and H120/R100 with validation R106; H139/R126 with validation R133. The EMT interaction for PTEN-loss STAT3, H195/R176, is discovery-positive but direct PTEN-loss strata are already robust, making it low priority. The very-high screen-quality MDM2 estimate H207/R187 is uncertain but broad screen-quality halves H226/R200 and H229/R206 support the main CDKN2A-loss MDM2 axis. The serum-rich KEAP1 interaction H269/R240 is a small borderline modifier and does not challenge the core KEAP1-NFE2L2 association. The most notable unresolved direct predictor is RB1 loss for STAT3 dependency: H266/R237 +0.039, +0.003 to +0.074; PTEN-intact H271/R242 +0.031, +0.004 to +0.057; CDKN2A-intact H273/R244 +0.046, +0.004 to +0.088. These are directionally consistent but modest, with lower bounds near null, correlated genomic confounders, and no validation capacity, so they remain unresolved possible small dataset-specific associations rather than accepted findings. The RB1-by-PTEN interaction H272/R243 is null and does not suggest modification of the accepted PTEN-loss STAT3 axis. Technical unresolved claims H236-H243 and likely H244-H246 are artifacts of invalid continuous-threshold coding or unevaluated continuous comparisons, not biological nulls.\\n\\nMost informative next analysis. Because independent validation capacity is exhausted, further work should be discovery-level and focused on either completing technically valid context robustness or clarifying the small RB1-STAT3 signal. If another analysis window is available, the highest-yield registered follow-up would test RB1-loss STAT3 dependency in the joint PTEN-intact and CDKN2A-intact population, as a valid refinement of H266, to see whether the small effect persists when the two strongest known STAT3 dependency modifiers are held fixed. A secondary priority is to avoid continuous-exposure claims and re-examine accepted axes through valid eligibility/subgroup contrasts only. No conclusion should be interpreted as causal, therapeutic, synthetic-lethal in vivo, or externally generalizable beyond this supplied dataset and its screen contexts.",
